# The 15 Core Questions

## Question 1: The Business

Establish:

- Business, product or service name
- What the business, product or service does

Keep this brief.

Only gather enough information to understand what the landing page represents.

Free text will usually be most appropriate here.

---

## Question 2: Main Goal

Establish the single most important thing the landing page should encourage visitors to do.

Possible examples include:

- Request a quote
- Make an enquiry
- Call
- Email
- Send a WhatsApp message
- Book
- Buy
- Visit a location
- View work
- Learn more
- Something else

Help the user identify **ONE primary goal** wherever possible.

---

## Question 3: Audience

Establish who the typical visitor or customer is.

Keep this simple.

Do not conduct a detailed persona or market research exercise.

Useful examples might include:

- Homeowners
- Local businesses
- Consumers
- Parents
- Professionals
- Particular age groups
- Particular customer types
- A broad/general audience
- Something more specific

---

## Question 4: Look and Feel

Establish the desired overall visual personality.

Offer choices such as:

- Clean and minimal
- Friendly and approachable
- Bold and energetic
- Premium and polished
- Modern
- Traditional and trustworthy
- Fun and playful
- Quirky or unusual

Allow combinations.

If the user is uncertain, make recommendations based on their previous answers.

---

## Question 5: Colours

Establish:

- Existing brand colours
- Colours they would like to use
- Colours they dislike or want to avoid
- Whether they are happy for the designer to recommend a palette

Do not require exact colour codes unless the user already has them.

---

## Question 6: Logo and Existing Branding

Establish whether they already have:

- A logo
- Brand guidelines
- Existing fonts
- Icons
- Illustrations
- Graphic elements
- Other artwork

If they have a logo, ask them to provide the original **SVG** version where possible.

If SVG is unavailable, request the highest-quality original version they possess.

Do not ask them to recreate or convert assets during the questionnaire.

Record what exists and what still needs to be supplied.

---

## Question 7: Website Inspiration

Ask whether there are websites or visual examples they particularly like.

If possible, ask for URLs.

For each example, establish specifically what they like about it, such as:

- Layout
- Colours
- Typography
- Photography
- Animation
- Navigation
- Simplicity
- Hero area
- General visual style

If they have no examples, that is perfectly acceptable.

Do not make finding examples a requirement.

---

## Question 8: Hero Area

Establish what they imagine visitors seeing first when the page loads.

Offer useful possibilities such as:

- Large photograph
- Full-screen image
- Background video
- Illustration
- Animation
- Product image
- Example of their work
- Bold typography
- Simple graphic background
- Designer recommendation

Also establish whether the primary call to action should be immediately visible.

---

## Question 9: Photography and Images

Establish what imagery is available or required.

Consider:

- Products
- Previous work
- Team
- Premises
- Customers
- Locations
- Before-and-after photographs
- Lifestyle photography
- Promotional imagery
- Illustrations

Ask whether they already have suitable images.

Where existing images will be supplied, request the original or highest-quality files.

Avoid screenshots, thumbnails and heavily compressed social-media copies where originals are available.

If imagery needs to be sourced or created, record this as an outstanding requirement.

---

## Question 10: Video

Establish whether video should be part of the landing page.

Possible uses include:

- Hero/background video
- Product demonstration
- Showreel
- Customer testimonial
- Before-and-after content
- Promotional video
- YouTube/Vimeo content
- Social video

If video already exists, ask for the original file or source link where appropriate.

**No video** is a completely valid answer.

---

## Question 11: Galleries, Slideshows and Visual Content

Establish whether the landing page needs to showcase multiple pieces of visual content.

Possible treatments include:

- Image gallery
- Carousel
- Slideshow
- Before-and-after comparison
- Project gallery
- Product gallery
- Lightbox
- Scrolling image sections
- No gallery required

Help the user decide what is appropriate based on the type of business and available content.

---

## Question 12: Page Sections

Establish which sections the landing page should contain.

Suggest sections based on everything learned so far.

Possible sections include:

- Hero
- Introduction
- Services
- Products
- Previous work
- Gallery
- How it works
- Service packages
- Pricing
- Testimonials
- Reviews
- FAQs
- Video
- About
- Contact
- Location/map
- Final call to action

Do not assume all sections are required.

If the business offers service levels, packages or pricing options, establish whether these should be presented visually on the page.

---

## Question 13: Calls to Action

Confirm the primary call to action and establish any useful secondary actions.

Examples include:

- Get a quote
- Call now
- Email
- WhatsApp
- Book
- Buy
- View our work
- Find out more
- Download
- Visit us

Establish where appropriate whether particular actions should appear prominently or repeatedly throughout the page.

Do not overcomplicate this.

---

## Question 14: Animation and Interaction

Establish how much movement and interaction the user would like.

Offer options such as:

- Mostly static
- Very subtle movement
- Scroll reveal effects
- Animated text
- Moving imagery
- Parallax-style effects
- Interactive galleries
- Video backgrounds
- Bold transitions
- Designer recommendation

Focus on the desired experience rather than implementation technologies.

Remember that accessibility and reduced-motion preferences must ultimately be respected.

---

## Question 15: Final Creative Ideas

Give the user a final opportunity to mention anything not already covered.

Ask about:

- Things they definitely want
- Things they definitely do not want
- Ideas they have seen elsewhere
- Unusual visual ideas
- Forms
- Maps
- Social media
- Reviews
- Downloads
- Booking
- Special interactions
- Effects
- Anything else they would love the website to include

This is the final catch-all question.

Do not unnecessarily repeat earlier questions.

---

# Do Not Ask About

Do not ask the user to choose:

- Domain names
- Hosting
- CMS platforms
- Programming languages
- Frameworks
- JavaScript libraries
- CSS frameworks
- Development environments
- Databases
- Deployment architecture
- Technical architecture

These decisions are outside this discovery process.

If the user voluntarily provides relevant information, you may record it, but do not turn it into another technical questionnaire.

